WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.270
-We are here with David Chase,
everybody,

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:06.973
the Emmy and Peabody-winning
writer and creator of

00:00:06.973 --> 00:00:09.809
"The Sopranos," which premiered
25 years ago today

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:11.143
and is streaming on Max.

00:00:11.143 --> 00:00:13.612
David, it is such a pleasure
to speak to you again.

00:00:13.612 --> 00:00:15.715
I would imagine a lot of people
are asking you this.

00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:18.084
25 years to the day
it premiered,

00:00:18.084 --> 00:00:20.954
did you have any sense that you
were going to make a show

00:00:20.954 --> 00:00:22.488
that people would still
be talking about

00:00:22.488 --> 00:00:24.356
this much
a quarter century later?

00:00:24.356 --> 00:00:26.492
-No, of course not. Not at all.

00:00:26.492 --> 00:00:28.294
-Did you have any confidence
in it

00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:31.330
at any point in the process?

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:34.134
-Well, yeah, five years in.
-Five years in?

00:00:34.134 --> 00:00:36.335
-Yeah.
-But what about premiere day?

00:00:36.335 --> 00:00:39.472
At the time, I feel,
you know, HBO was a thing.

00:00:39.472 --> 00:00:41.140
We knew it was
prestige television.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:43.309
I don't think we'd quite seen
anything like "The Sopranos."

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:45.911
Did HBO think they had
a hit on their hands?

00:00:45.911 --> 00:00:48.881
-Well, I got a call --

00:00:48.881 --> 00:00:51.818
It was going to happen
on January 10th,

00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:53.019
and I got a call from a woman

00:00:53.019 --> 00:00:54.787
whose name I wish
I could remember, who said,

00:00:54.787 --> 00:00:59.793
"Listen, we have two kinds
of parties here on HBO.

00:00:59.793 --> 00:01:02.528
There's a normal party
at series debuts,

00:01:02.528 --> 00:01:07.800
cast, crew, and family,
a nice restaurant."

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.702
I said, "Oh, that sounds good."

00:01:09.702 --> 00:01:12.638
And she said, "Then there's
another kind of party,

00:01:12.638 --> 00:01:15.674
at a better restaurant,
at a big screening room,

00:01:15.674 --> 00:01:17.076
a whole other thing."

00:01:17.076 --> 00:01:20.513
And I thought, "Holy [bleep]."

00:01:20.513 --> 00:01:21.780
And that's what happened.
-Wow.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.249
So they upgraded you
to the big premiere.

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:24.483
-They upgraded me
to the big premiere.

00:01:24.483 --> 00:01:25.517
-That's very exciting.

00:01:25.517 --> 00:01:27.053
And the reviews early on,

00:01:27.053 --> 00:01:30.556
everybody, almost to a
publication, loved it.

00:01:30.556 --> 00:01:31.724
"Hollywood Reporter,"

00:01:31.724 --> 00:01:33.993
"David Chase rises
to the challenge

00:01:33.993 --> 00:01:35.528
with incredibly
absorbing scripts

00:01:35.528 --> 00:01:37.230
and the help
of a remarkable cast."

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.531
"Newsday," "It's a great show.
The best new series of the year.

00:01:39.531 --> 00:01:41.167
It's so,
dare I say it, original,

00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:42.502
it catches you off guard."

00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:44.704
"The Orlando Sentinel"
stands alone.

00:01:44.704 --> 00:01:46.939
-Right. That's right.
-You remember this?

00:01:46.939 --> 00:01:48.741
-I do remember this. Mm-hmm.
Of course I remember this.

00:01:48.741 --> 00:01:51.210
-"A gangster's
midlife crisis is a weak,

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:54.280
unpalatable premise
for a series.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:57.417
'The Sopranos' makes you
an offer you can refuse."

00:01:57.417 --> 00:01:59.719
[ Laughter ]
-Right.

00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:02.021
And one other journalist said

00:02:02.021 --> 00:02:06.559
that they found that guy
in a barrel in Miami Bay.

00:02:06.559 --> 00:02:09.328
[ Laughter ]

00:02:09.328 --> 00:02:11.598
-You know,
I've watched this show

00:02:11.598 --> 00:02:14.066
from beginning to end
multiple times now,

00:02:14.066 --> 00:02:17.169
and it becomes so much,
through the series,

00:02:17.169 --> 00:02:19.305
so much more fun
to watch everyone,

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:21.540
because you learn
who the characters are

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:24.210
and their peccadilloes
and the way they talk.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:27.280
Did they become more fun to
write for, the longer it went?

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:29.048
Were there characters that you
were delighted to know,

00:02:29.048 --> 00:02:31.049
you had a scene
with so and so?

00:02:31.049 --> 00:02:32.318
-Oh, yeah, definitely.

00:02:32.318 --> 00:02:35.288
-Did you have a favorite
person to write for?

00:02:35.288 --> 00:02:37.056
-I guess I would say Junior.

00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:38.490
-Yeah. Junior was great.

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:40.059
-Junior was just, I don't know.

00:02:40.059 --> 00:02:43.629
-Well, first of all,

00:02:43.629 --> 00:02:45.965
Olivia was everybody's,
every writer's favorite.

00:02:45.965 --> 00:02:47.199
-Yes, Tony's mom.

00:02:47.199 --> 00:02:49.569
-And then after she was gone,
it became Junior.

00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:51.403
And I mean,
everybody was great,

00:02:51.403 --> 00:02:53.639
but Junior had something
going for him,

00:02:53.639 --> 00:02:56.675
and Paulie, you know?

00:02:56.675 --> 00:02:59.745
-And the thing about it is the
show is obviously very serious.

00:02:59.745 --> 00:03:01.814
People were very invested in
what happened to the characters,

00:03:01.814 --> 00:03:05.618
the fates of everybody,
but it was so funny as well.

00:03:05.618 --> 00:03:08.688
Did you feel you were
writing a drama?

00:03:08.688 --> 00:03:10.123
Did you feel you were
writing a comedy,

00:03:10.123 --> 00:03:12.658
or did it just switch based on
the scene and the character?

00:03:12.658 --> 00:03:16.028
-Well, I remember being
at college up in the Bronx

00:03:16.028 --> 00:03:18.097
and going to
somebody's funeral,

00:03:18.097 --> 00:03:22.101
and on the way, I passed another
Italian funeral going on,

00:03:22.101 --> 00:03:24.837
and there was these women
on their knees going,

00:03:24.837 --> 00:03:25.972
[ Imitates sobbing ]

00:03:25.972 --> 00:03:28.140
in front of the coffin.
"Oh, my God."

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.710
And I knew that
those were paid mourners.

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:36.082
And I thought, "This is
horrible and funny."

00:03:36.082 --> 00:03:37.317
[ Laughter ]

00:03:37.317 --> 00:03:39.552
So I thought,
"That's what I want to do."

00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:41.253
-Yeah.

00:03:41.253 --> 00:03:42.388
Well, you accomplished it.

00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:43.556
Yet we talked about

00:03:43.556 --> 00:03:45.692
how the confidence
came in Season Five.

00:03:45.692 --> 00:03:48.527
You did not, even
with the critical praise,

00:03:48.527 --> 00:03:51.897
you did not think it would
receive a second season?

00:03:51.897 --> 00:03:53.399
-No, I didn't.

00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:58.437
Because a show that survives
on TV,

00:03:58.437 --> 00:04:00.740
it's a tiny, tiny bullseye

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:02.675
in the middle of a target,
you know?

00:04:02.675 --> 00:04:05.711
Edie Falco said to me --

00:04:05.711 --> 00:04:09.215
and also, we were done with
the whole show before it aired.

00:04:09.215 --> 00:04:11.184
That's not the way it usually
works on network TV.

00:04:11.184 --> 00:04:13.686
You're working on it
while it's on the air.

00:04:13.686 --> 00:04:15.555
So Edie said to me,
"So what happens now?"

00:04:15.555 --> 00:04:18.123
And I said, "Well, not much.

00:04:18.123 --> 00:04:20.459
I think we've done it
and we had a really good time,

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:21.660
and it was great."

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:23.762
And she said, "Why?"

00:04:23.762 --> 00:04:25.832
I said, "It's just not
going to happen.

00:04:25.832 --> 00:04:28.101
It doesn't ever happen, really."

00:04:28.101 --> 00:04:31.337
And she said,
"Well, we had so much fun."

00:04:31.337 --> 00:04:34.840
I said, "No,
they don't want you to have fun.

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:38.510
That's not the purpose.
That's the problem."

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:41.047
-You would make
a terrible cheerleader.

00:04:43.149 --> 00:04:47.053
-I tried out in high school.
-You tried out in high school?

00:04:47.053 --> 00:04:50.256
-They said, "You know, we got
a job on the paid mourner team."

00:04:50.256 --> 00:04:51.858
-That's right.

00:04:51.858 --> 00:04:53.792
But they didn't pay enough.
-They didn't pay enough.

00:04:53.792 --> 00:04:56.796
But you also, it's interesting
because, you know, again,

00:04:56.796 --> 00:04:59.499
I think a lot of the people
in the show had worked before.

00:04:59.499 --> 00:05:01.134
It's what they became known for.

00:05:01.134 --> 00:05:02.768
You did have a career
in television,

00:05:02.768 --> 00:05:04.003
so you'd been
through this before.

00:05:04.003 --> 00:05:05.938
You'd worked on shows
that succeeded,

00:05:05.938 --> 00:05:08.841
like "The Rockford Files,"
"Northern Exposure."

00:05:08.841 --> 00:05:11.744
But you also had some ideas
before "The Sopranos"

00:05:11.744 --> 00:05:13.779
of shows that did not succeed,

00:05:13.779 --> 00:05:17.783
including
a Superman-inspired show.

00:05:17.783 --> 00:05:18.951
-Yes. That's true.

00:05:18.951 --> 00:05:20.219
-What was the name
of your Superman show?

00:05:20.219 --> 00:05:22.488
-The Superman-inspired show
was called "Ultimo."

00:05:22.488 --> 00:05:23.622
-"Ultimo."

00:05:23.622 --> 00:05:27.126
-And it was about
an Italian-American kid

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:29.795
whose father was

00:05:29.795 --> 00:05:32.264
a scientist
at the Houston Space Lab,

00:05:32.264 --> 00:05:34.933
and his father took him
to work on a Saturday,

00:05:34.933 --> 00:05:37.036
and the kid ate
some little moon rocks,

00:05:37.036 --> 00:05:40.072
and it never came out
in his stool.

00:05:40.072 --> 00:05:45.044
And he got these superpowers,
and then

00:05:45.044 --> 00:05:49.514
he had to start hiring
publicity people,

00:05:49.514 --> 00:05:54.153
legal people, had a whole
Tuesday morning staff meeting,

00:05:54.153 --> 00:05:56.856
because in the pilot,
he was being sued

00:05:56.856 --> 00:06:00.192
because he took a big boulder
off a mountain

00:06:00.192 --> 00:06:04.430
and smashed
some terrorists in a cabin.

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:05.898
And that caused vibration,

00:06:05.898 --> 00:06:07.967
which caused a ski lift
to fall down,

00:06:07.967 --> 00:06:09.668
and a lot of people
were seriously injured.

00:06:09.668 --> 00:06:12.504
So he was being sued
by all those people.

00:06:12.504 --> 00:06:16.809
-And how did the network
receive this script?

00:06:16.809 --> 00:06:18.945
-The way they received
all of them.

00:06:18.945 --> 00:06:20.546
[ Laughter ]

00:06:20.546 --> 00:06:23.582
-You did not,
going into "The Sopranos,"

00:06:23.582 --> 00:06:26.385
you preferred film
as a medium to television.

00:06:26.385 --> 00:06:29.422
And I think you started, or it
was certainly one of the shows

00:06:29.422 --> 00:06:32.391
that started this shift to a far
more cinematic television.

00:06:32.391 --> 00:06:35.494
Was that one of the things
you set out to do?

00:06:35.494 --> 00:06:37.630
-Yes. It was, yeah.

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:39.431
-And was HBO amenable to this?

00:06:39.431 --> 00:06:40.866
Was that something you had
to explain to them?

00:06:40.866 --> 00:06:42.301
-No, I did not.

00:06:42.301 --> 00:06:45.637
I've said it before,
HBO was like --

00:06:45.637 --> 00:06:50.509
I was all set to do
another development deal at Fox

00:06:50.509 --> 00:06:53.012
and to be at the executive
producer of "Millennium."

00:06:53.012 --> 00:06:54.713
Do you remember that show?
-Yeah, I remember that show.

00:06:54.713 --> 00:07:00.152
And I went
to a meeting over there,

00:07:00.152 --> 00:07:04.423
and then I had another meeting
around the corner at HBO,

00:07:04.423 --> 00:07:08.694
and I said to HBO,
"Well, can I direct a pilot?

00:07:08.694 --> 00:07:10.730
Because if I can,
then I'll do it."

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:13.299
And they said yes,
so that other one,

00:07:13.299 --> 00:07:16.034
which was a contract signing
meeting, never happened.

00:07:16.034 --> 00:07:18.938
-Wow, that's amazing
how a very small --

00:07:18.938 --> 00:07:20.706
Always have two meetings
in the same day.

00:07:20.706 --> 00:07:22.475
That's the real...
-Yeah.

00:07:22.475 --> 00:07:25.444
-We've had cast members,
I've read interviews.

00:07:25.444 --> 00:07:27.213
I had cast members here
who've talked about

00:07:27.213 --> 00:07:29.615
how actual members of the mob

00:07:29.615 --> 00:07:32.084
had reached out to them
during the filming of the show,

00:07:32.084 --> 00:07:35.421
after the filming of the show,
because they wanted to,

00:07:35.421 --> 00:07:37.023
you know, just talk to people
who were on "The Sopranos."

00:07:37.023 --> 00:07:39.391
I think that was probably a show
that was interesting to them.

00:07:39.391 --> 00:07:44.696
Did members of the actual
Mafia ever try to contact you?

00:07:44.696 --> 00:07:49.168
-No. Although,
I have to be careful here.

00:07:49.168 --> 00:07:51.436
-Yeah, I have to be.

00:07:51.436 --> 00:07:55.173
Make sure it's only a one-shot.

00:07:55.173 --> 00:07:58.877
-Yeah, yeah, I have...

00:07:58.877 --> 00:08:00.779
I think he's gone now.

00:08:00.779 --> 00:08:02.848
I had a cousin by marriage

00:08:02.848 --> 00:08:06.085
who was married to an older
cousin of mine, a woman.

00:08:06.085 --> 00:08:09.788
And I never went there,
for obvious reasons,

00:08:09.788 --> 00:08:13.492
And right toward the end,
maybe fifth or sixth season,

00:08:13.492 --> 00:08:16.262
my wife and I
had dinner with those two,

00:08:16.262 --> 00:08:18.697
and we had a night
in Clifton, New Jersey,

00:08:18.697 --> 00:08:20.499
two minutes from
where I grew up,

00:08:20.499 --> 00:08:21.667
and we had a nice dinner.

00:08:21.667 --> 00:08:24.604
And, he and I said,
"You know what?

00:08:24.604 --> 00:08:28.107
Let's forget all this [bleep].
Let's you and I have lunch."

00:08:28.107 --> 00:08:31.576
We made a lunch date for
two Thursdays from now,

00:08:31.576 --> 00:08:35.181
and starting on Wednesday.
I started thinking,

00:08:35.181 --> 00:08:38.985
"I don't know about this.
I don't know about this.

00:08:38.985 --> 00:08:40.819
I don't think
this is a good idea."

00:08:40.819 --> 00:08:43.522
And by Thursday,
I was pretty much agitated,

00:08:43.522 --> 00:08:47.693
and I almost had my hand on
the phone, and the phone rings.

00:08:47.693 --> 00:08:49.094
I answer the phone. It's him.

00:08:49.094 --> 00:08:52.063
He says,
"David, I've been thinking,

00:08:52.063 --> 00:08:54.199
I don't think we should
do this."

00:08:54.199 --> 00:08:57.135
So that was, you know --
-Probably for the best.

00:08:57.135 --> 00:08:58.837
-That was definitely
for the best,

00:08:58.837 --> 00:09:01.407
because he had already asked me
for Springsteen tickets.

00:09:01.407 --> 00:09:03.442
-Yeah.
-And, you know.

00:09:03.442 --> 00:09:05.144
-Yeah, you don't need people --

00:09:05.144 --> 00:09:06.478
If he's really in the Mafia,

00:09:06.478 --> 00:09:07.879
he can get his own
Springsteen tickets.

00:09:07.879 --> 00:09:10.216
-Well, you would think so.

00:09:10.216 --> 00:09:12.818
I mean, it was that
and some other stuff.

00:09:12.818 --> 00:09:14.987
It had already started.
-Yeah.

00:09:14.987 --> 00:09:17.089
-It's hard enough
having a cousin by marriage.

00:09:17.089 --> 00:09:18.190
You don't need them
also in the mob.

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:21.093
-Right,
but that cousin is the guy

00:09:21.093 --> 00:09:24.797
that told me and Terry Winter
about a guy from Newark

00:09:24.797 --> 00:09:27.066
who shot his wife
in the beehive hairdo.

00:09:27.066 --> 00:09:28.501
-Oh, really?
-That's where that came from,

00:09:28.501 --> 00:09:30.002
from that cousin.
-See, that's why

00:09:30.002 --> 00:09:31.103
he couldn't keep talking to you.

00:09:31.103 --> 00:09:32.537
They would eventually be like,

00:09:32.537 --> 00:09:35.207
"Hey, these are
a lot of your stories."

00:09:35.207 --> 00:09:38.911
HBO put together
a quick clip package

00:09:38.911 --> 00:09:40.880
of "The Sopranos"
on its 25th anniversary.

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:42.448
Let's take a look.

00:09:42.448 --> 00:09:47.786
-I'd like to propose
a toast to my family.

00:09:47.786 --> 00:09:50.623
Someday soon, you're gonna
have families of your own,

00:09:50.623 --> 00:09:52.024
and if you're lucky,
you'll remember

00:09:52.024 --> 00:09:54.827
the little moments
that were good.

00:09:54.827 --> 00:10:06.672 line:0%
♪♪

00:10:06.672 --> 00:10:09.941
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:10:09.941 --> 00:10:14.380
-Is it strange to watch
a clip package?

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:16.848
-Uh...

00:10:16.848 --> 00:10:18.116
Seems very violent to me.

00:10:18.116 --> 00:10:19.584
-It's a very violent show.

00:10:19.584 --> 00:10:22.354
If you haven't seen it,
it's a very violent show.

00:10:22.354 --> 00:10:23.488
What an honor to have you here.

00:10:23.488 --> 00:10:25.191
Congratulations.
-Thank you.

00:10:25.191 --> 00:10:27.726
-David Chase.
"The Sopranos" streaming on Max.

00:10:27.726 --> 00:10:29.261
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

